{0 Audio}

The engine ships no audio components and no audio systems — {!Eon_engine.Audio_backend}
completes the {{!page-platform_and_loop}[Platform.S]} trifecta (alongside [Input_backend] and
[Rendering_backend]), and {!Eon_engine.Audio_command_buffer} is the shared,
game-owned accumulator any system can append to. What sound to play, when, and
from which game system, is entirely a game-layer decision.

{1 Audio_backend — the platform seam}

{[
module type S = sig
  val init     : unit -> unit
  val submit   : Audio_command.t list -> unit
  val shutdown : unit -> unit
end
]}

One function does the real work: [submit]. [init]/[shutdown] bracket the loop's
lifetime — [init] pre-loads sound files into backend-internal handles, [shutdown]
releases them. [Audio_backend.Null] discards every command; it's what
[Platform.Headless] uses for CI and tests.

{1 Audio_command — the vocabulary}

{[
open Eon_engine

type t =
  | Play_sound of {
      id          : string;
      instance_id : string option;
      volume      : float;
      pitch       : float;
      pan         : float;
      loop        : bool;
    }
  | Stop_sound        of string
  | Set_volume        of string * float
  | Set_pitch         of string * float
  | Set_pan           of string * float
  | Play_music        of { id : string; volume : float; loop : bool }
  | Stop_music
  | Set_music_volume  of float
  | Crossfade_music   of { id : string; duration : float }
  | Set_master_volume of float
  | Pause_all
  | Resume_all
  | Stop_all
]}

[instance_id] is what makes a sound addressable after it starts — set it for
anything you'll want to [Stop_sound]/[Set_volume]/[Set_pan] later (a looping
spatial emitter); leave it [None] for fire-and-forget one-shots (a hit sound, a
UI click) where the backend owns the voice lifetime.

{1 Audio_command_buffer — the shared accumulator}

{[
type t

val create  : unit -> t
val clear   : t -> unit
val add     : t -> Audio_command.t -> unit
val to_list : t -> Audio_command.t list

val fetch     : [> World.ro] World.t -> t   (** raises [Not_found] if unstored *)
val fetch_opt : [> World.ro] World.t -> t option
val store     : World.rw World.t -> t -> unit
]}

There is no dedicated engine-level audio system — any system that detects a
trigger fetches the buffer and appends:

{[
open Eon_engine

(* combat system detects a hit *)
let on_hit world =
  let buf = Audio_command_buffer.fetch (World.readonly world) in
  Audio_command_buffer.add buf
    (Audio_command.Play_sound
       { id = "hit_01"; instance_id = None;
         volume = 0.9; pitch = 1.0; pan = 0.0; loop = false })

(* spatial audio system updates a looping emitter each frame *)
let update_emitter world iid ~volume ~pan =
  let buf = Audio_command_buffer.fetch (World.readonly world) in
  Audio_command_buffer.add buf (Audio_command.Set_volume (iid, volume));
  Audio_command_buffer.add buf (Audio_command.Set_pan    (iid, pan))
]}

{1 Frame flow}

{[
  collect — Signals -> Events -> Commands
  tick    — Progress.tick; any system calls Audio_command_buffer.add
  drain   — Signals -> Commands -> Events
  loop    — Platform.Rendering_backend.render stream ~dt
            Platform.Audio_backend.submit (Audio_command_buffer.to_list buf)
            Audio_command_buffer.clear buf
]}

The buffer is cleared {i after} [submit], not before [tick] — the loop always
submits exactly what accumulated during the current frame. See
{{!page-platform_and_loop}[Loop.Make]} for the full per-frame sequence this fits into.

{2 Voice budget prioritization}

Backends have a finite voice budget (typically 32-64 simultaneous sounds). If a
game needs to cull low-priority requests before they reach the backend, a
dedicated system can run after all audio-producing systems, read the buffer via
[to_list], drop or reorder entries, and replace the buffer contents before the
loop calls [submit] — the shared buffer means this system sees every request
for the frame in one place.

{1 Triggering sounds: three patterns}

{table
  {tr {th Trigger} {th Example} {th Where the append happens}}
  {tr {td Event-driven}     {td Skill SFX, death sound, UI click} {td A signal handler or a system reacting to an event}}
  {tr {td Polled / spatial} {td Monster growl, ambient fire}      {td A system querying position each frame}}
  {tr {td Global state}     {td Music track change}               {td A system reading a music resource}}
}

One-shot audio triggers are local-only and never cross the network, the same
rule as input Signals — a remote client receives the game-state event that
caused the sound ([Enemy_died], [Skill_used]) and appends its own local sound
independently. Audio is never replicated; only game state is.

{1 Sound effects vs. music}

{table
  {tr {th} {th Sound effects} {th Music}}
  {tr {td Length}   {td Short (< 5s)}                    {td Long (minutes)}}
  {tr {td Memory}   {td Loaded fully into RAM as PCM}     {td Streamed from disk, decoded in chunks}}
  {tr {td Voices}   {td Regular voice slot}               {td Separate streaming voice}}
  {tr {td Format}   {td WAV preferred (no decode cost)}   {td OGG preferred (compression)}}
}

The backend handles the streaming/decoding difference internally — the engine
always sends the same [Play_sound]/[Play_music] commands regardless.

{1 Worked example: spatial audio}

A game that wants distance-attenuated looping sounds defines its own
[Sound_emitter] component (not shipped by the engine) plus a [Sound_listener]
{{!page-world_and_components}Resource} (world-scoped, one-per-world state —
not a per-entity component) and a system that queries emitters each frame.
[Query.Default] (not bare [Query]) is the pre-instantiated query instance;
[View.get] needs a first-class module; [Eon_ecs.Entity_id] has no
[to_string] — build an id from [index]/[generation] instead; [Resource.fetch]
returns the value directly, not an option — unlike the raw [World.get_data]
this replaces:

{[
open Eon_engine

module Sound_emitter = struct
  type t = {
    sound_id : string;
    range    : float;
    volume   : float;
    playing  : bool;
  }
  let component : t Components.t = Eon_engine.component "Sound_emitter"
  let name = Components.name component
end

type sound_listener = { position : Math.Vec2.t }

module Sound_listener = Resource.Make (struct
  type t = sound_listener
  let key = Resource.key `Sound_listener
end)

let emitter_instance_id view =
  let e = View.entity view in
  Printf.sprintf "emitter:%d:%d" (Eon_ecs.Entity_id.index e) (Eon_ecs.Entity_id.generation e)

let update (world : World.ro World.t) _dt =
  let buf      = Audio_command_buffer.fetch world in
  let listener = Sound_listener.fetch world in
  Query.Default.from world
  |> Query.Default.having Sound_emitter.name
  |> Query.Default.iter (fun view ->
       let emitter = View.get view (module Sound_emitter) in
       let pos     = View.get view (module Components.Local_transform) in
       let iid     = emitter_instance_id view in
       let dist    = Math.Vec2.distance listener.position pos.position in
       if dist > emitter.range then begin
         if emitter.playing then
           Audio_command_buffer.add buf (Audio_command.Stop_sound iid)
       end else begin
         let vol = emitter.volume *. (1.0 -. (dist /. emitter.range)) in
         if not emitter.playing then
           Audio_command_buffer.add buf
             (Audio_command.Play_sound
                { id = emitter.sound_id; instance_id = Some iid;
                  volume = vol; pitch = 1.0; pan = 0.0; loop = true })
         else
           Audio_command_buffer.add buf (Audio_command.Set_volume (iid, vol))
       end)
]}

This reads [Sound_emitter]/[Local_transform] and a resource, and only
appends to a buffer — it never writes the world — so it belongs in a
[Parallel] system's [update], per
{{!page-queries_and_systems}Queries and systems}.

For the full design rationale (why audio differs from rendering/input, backend
selection, and what NOT to do) see [docs/design/audio_system_design.md] in the
repository.

